A||||5||GT Bastard||Which flywheel for the 3.1, 3.2?||||1||
Z||000000||GT Bastard||07-14-2000||09:59 PM||||Would the Flywheel from my 2.8 be useable on the 3.1?  I've heard a couple different things, and I want to get this right.  3.1's are internally balanced as well, right? So do I go w/o the harmonic balancer?  TIA guys...||||reg||1
Z||000001||Cooter||07-14-2000||10:18 PM||||You need the flywheel and balancer for an 88 up 2.8 or 3.1- I still have a new GM manual tranny flywheel I did not use for my 3.4 for sale!||||reg||1
Z||000002||GT Bastard||07-14-2000||10:39 PM||||So I CAN use my existing flywheel?||||reg||1
Z||000003||PJ||07-14-2000||10:49 PM||||I believe the internally balanced crank was introduced in '87. The giveaway is the big circular timing disk in the middle of the crank. So an '87 flywheel and balancer should work as well. Look if the the flywheel is off-weighted on the motor side (of the flywheel). If it is its wrong. I can post a part # for the balanced flywheel tommorrow if you need it. Either way you'll still need the balancer.<P>PJ<BR>ITA #66 Black||||reg||1
Z||000004||lowCG||07-15-2000||12:17 AM||||The Fiero flywheels with a counterweight can be nuetrally ballanced to do the job on an internally balanced motor.You need to machine away the counterweight,then take it to a machine shop that can verify/balance it for you.Mine cost me $70 with a re-surface.||||reg||1
Z||000005||HYPTNOTiSE||07-15-2000||12:24 AM||||I know what the deal is on these, been there done that.  I'm going to use an 88 flywheel, which is THE only stock fiero flywheel you can use with a 3.1 crank.  You can have the counter weight ground off, and the flywheel balanced. That works too.  Also you can use a flywheel from any GM FWD 5 speed car that uses a 3.1 or 3.4 engine. <P>------------------<BR>Mike LeCompte<BR>86 Fiero GT 3.2L w/ nitrous||||reg||1
